I’ll check the clocking semantics, then derive occupancy lemmas for the count bound.The key strengthening is **occupancy consistency**: `count` equals the number of busy entries. Under the benchmark’s intended atomic, pre-edge transition model, this makes the bound inductive; the other lemmas expose the allocation and free cases to the prover. The RTL also has a caveat: blocking assignments in separate clocked processes can interleave in event-driven simulation, so these lemmas are **not guaranteed for every legal simulation schedule** without resolving that race. ([cs.ox.ac.uk](https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/rajdeep.mukherjee/tacas-appendix.pdf))

<json>
{
  "lemmas": [
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_1; @(posedge clk) (count == $countones({busy[15], busy[14], busy[13], busy[12], busy[11], busy[10], busy[9], busy[8], busy[7], busy[6], busy[5], busy[4], busy[3], busy[2], busy[1], busy[0]})); endproperty",
      "explanation": "The initialized count and occupancy are both zero. Each accepted allocation adds one busy entry, and each free of a busy entry removes one. Thus count cannot exceed the 16-entry occupancy."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_2; @(posedge clk) (count == 5'd16) |-> (&{busy[15], busy[14], busy[13], busy[12], busy[11], busy[10], busy[9], busy[8], busy[7], busy[6], busy[5], busy[4], busy[3], busy[2], busy[1], busy[0]}); endproperty",
      "explanation": "Identifies the full-queue case explicitly, helping the prover show that nack prevents another count increment."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_3; @(posedge clk) (count < 5'd16) |-> !busy[alloc_addr]; endproperty",
      "explanation": "With fewer than 16 busy entries, the priority selector chooses a vacant entry; an accepted allocation therefore accounts for a new busy bit."
    },
    {
      "lemma": "property lemma_4; @(posedge clk) busy[free_addr] |-> (count != 5'd0); endproperty",
      "explanation": "A busy entry implies positive occupancy, preventing a successful free from underflowing the five-bit count."
    }
  ]
}
</json>

Under a coherent clock-to-clock interpretation of the module, the key strengthening is that `count` equals the number of busy entries.

